<header class="skibby-hero"><p class="section-label">Giro d’Italia · 1954</p><h1>The 16-year-old mechanic<br/>who became <em>two years older</em> overnight</h1><p>Ivo Faltoni could build wheels. But he was too young to follow the Giro.</p></header>
<figure class="skibby-image"><img src={`${base}/images/stories/ivo-faltoni-giro-1954/illustration.webp`} alt="Illustration of a young bicycle mechanic truing a wheel in a temporary workshop before the 1954 Giro d’Italia"/><figcaption>Illustration of a young mechanic before the 1954 Giro d’Italia. AI-generated; not a historical photograph.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="skibby-opening"><p class="section-label">Palermo</p><p>Ivo Faltoni had travelled all the way to the Giro start when the problem emerged. He was only 16.</p></section>
<section class="skibby-story"><aside><strong>1954</strong><span>Wheels, craft<br/>and proof of age</span></aside><div>
<p>Faltoni could build wheels, and Gino Bartali’s team needed a mechanic. But 16 was too young to be allowed into the Giro d’Italia service caravan. The race was due to begin the next day.</p><p>Many years later, Faltoni said that journalist Dante Garioni told him to sleep peacefully. By the following morning, the 16-year-old was 18 on paper. A false document had achieved what experience and good hands could not: admitting him officially to the race.</p>
<h2>A recommendation from a domestique</h2><p>Faltoni came from the area around Cortona in Tuscany and was already known as a good wheelbuilder. It may sound like a narrow skill, but a hand-built wheel requires evenly tensioned spokes so that the rim runs true and withstands the load. On Italian roads in 1954, that mattered.</p><p>About ten days before the Giro, Bartali lost one of his mechanics to illness. The sick man’s name has not been found in the sources checked. Several later accounts, however, identify domestique Lido Sartini as the man who recommended Faltoni. Sartini knew a young Tuscan who was particularly good with wheels.</p><p>That was enough to send Faltoni south.</p><p>Bartali was then 39 and riding for his own Bartali–Brooklyn team. He had won the Giro three times and the Tour de France twice. The 1954 Giro would be his last, but the work around him remained as it always had. Bicycles had to be washed, checked and prepared again. Wheels needed truing and building. Tyres, chains, brakes and gears had to last another day.</p>
<h2>Eighteen on paper</h2><p>When the team reached Palermo, Faltoni was, according to his later account, rejected because he was a minor. The Giro was a travelling workplace lasting more than three weeks. Mechanics followed the peloton in cars, worked late and rose early again.</p><p>In an Italian newspaper story from 2011, Faltoni remembered the night this way: he went to bed aged 16 and woke aged 18, confirmed by a well-made false document. The story was told long after the Giro and should be treated as his recollection, not a contemporary report.</p><blockquote>Faltoni was old enough to be trusted with Bartali’s wheels, but too young to appear on the entry list as who he really was.</blockquote><p>The main outline is supported by several sources: Faltoni began working as Bartali’s mechanic at 16, and 1954 was the year he joined the team at the Giro.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="skibby-facts">{facts.map(([number,label])=><div><strong>{number}</strong><span>{label}</span></div>)}</section>
<section class="skibby-context"><div><p class="section-label">Three weeks behind the peloton</p><h2>The mechanics’ race began after the finish</h2></div><div><p>The Giro began in Palermo on 21 May and ended in Milan on 13 June. The official history records 4,337 kilometres, 105 starters and 67 finishers. Bartali–Brooklyn entered seven riders. Bartali himself completed his final Giro d’Italia in 13th place.</p><p>Faltoni was not part of the result. His race took place behind it.</p><p>A mechanic did not merely repair what had already broken. The job was equally to find the next problem before the rider did. A loose spoke, a worn brake block or a slightly damaged tyre could prove costly on the following stage. When the riders finished, the mechanics’ evening work began.</p><p>The surviving sources do not describe each of Faltoni’s repairs day by day, so they are not reconstructed here. We know that he was recruited for his ability as a mechanic and wheelbuilder, that he accompanied the team during the Giro, and that the trip lasted about three weeks.</p></div></section>
<section class="skibby-return"><p class="section-label">Bartali’s final Giro</p><blockquote>Faltoni received no placing. His work began when the riders had finished.</blockquote><div><p>In sporting terms, the 1954 race did not belong to Bartali. Switzerland’s Carlo Clerici won ahead of Hugo Koblet and Nino Assirelli. Bartali finished more than 50 minutes behind, won no stage and ended his professional career later that year.</p><p>For Faltoni, the Giro began a long friendship with Bartali. Sources use different descriptions of his later role: personal mechanic, employee, friend and confidant. What is certain is that their connection continued long after his single season as a professional mechanic.</p></div></section>
<section class="skibby-finish"><div><p class="section-label">A job that stayed with him</p><h2>From young wheelbuilder to Bartali’s old mechanic</h2></div><div><p>Faltoni died in 2020 at the age of 82. When photographed as an old man among the mementoes of a life in cycling, he was still introduced as Bartali’s former mechanic. It was a short employment that stayed with him.</p><p>There are bigger stories from the 1954 Giro. Clerici’s long breakaway transformed the standings. The peloton later protested on the road to St Moritz. Coppi, Koblet and Magni were still on the start list. But at the edge of it all stood a boy who was good at building wheels and two years too young.</p><p>It was settled in Palermo. Not with a new rule, but with a new date of birth.</p></div></section>
<section class="period-sources"><p class="section-label">Sources and image note</p><div><p>The illustration is a contemporary, AI-generated interpretation and is not used as historical documentation. The story of the false document comes from Faltoni’s later recollection. The sick mechanic whom he replaced has not yet been identified.</p><ul><li><a href="https://www.gazzetta.it/Speciali/Giroditalia/archivio-storico/it/edizioni/edizione_39.shtml">Giro d’Italia: official historical archive for 1954</a></li><li><a href="https://www.cyclingnews.com/features/gino-bartali-tribute-ride-gallery/">Cyclingnews: meeting Ivo Faltoni, 2018</a></li><li><a href="https://www.cinquantamila.it/storyTellerArticolo.php?storyId=0000001394405">Cinquantamila: Faltoni’s recollection of the 1954 Giro</a></li><li><a href="https://www.ancoraonline.it/2015/06/28/proseguono-i-festeggiamenti-in-onore-del-beato-pier-giorgio-frassatti/">L’Ancora: Faltoni and Bartali’s team</a></li><li><a href="https://www.letruria.it/download/3/2363_2010-01.pdf">L’Etruria: Faltoni’s recollection of Sartini and the Giro</a></li><li><a href="https://www.lanazione.it/arezzo/cronaca/laddio-a-ivo-faltoni-amico-dei-grandi-del-ciclismo-custodi-il-segreto-di-bartali-dk2bisrg">La Nazione: obituary for Ivo Faltoni</a></li></ul></div></section>
